>Brown</surname><given-names>AF</given-names></name><name><surname>Ettner</surname><given-names>SL</given-names></name><name><surname>Piette</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Weinberger</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Gregg</surname><given-names>E</given-names></name><name><surname>Shapiro</surname><given-names>MF</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><year>2004</year><volume>26</volume><fpage>63</fpage><lpage>77</lpage><article-title>Socioeconomic position and health among persons with diabetes mellitus: a conceptual framework and review of the literature</article-title><source>Epidemiol Rev</source><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15234948</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B10"><label>10</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Krieger</surname><given-names>N</given-names></name></person-group><volume>53</volume><issue>11</issue><year>1999</year><fpage>678</fpage><lpage>680</lpage><article-title>Sticky webs, hungry spiders, buzzing flies, and fractal metaphors: on the misleading juxtaposition of "risk factor" versus "social" epidemiology</article-title><source>J Epidemiol Community Health</source><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10656092</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B11"><label>11</label><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Salmon</surname><given-names>CT</given-names></name></person-group><person-group person-group-type="editor"><name><surname>Deerta</surname><given-names>SA</given-names></name></person-group><year>1992</year><publisher-loc>Newbury Park (CA)</publisher-loc><publisher-name>SAGE Publications</publisher-name><article-title>Bridging theory "of" and theory "for" communication campaigns. An essay on ideology and public policy</article-title><source>Communication yearbook</source></citation></ref><ref id="B12"><label>12</label><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Smith</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><name><surname>Williams</surname><given-names>DG</given-names></name><name><surname>Johnson</surname><given-names>NA</given-names></name></person-group><year>1999</year><publisher-loc>New York (NY)</publisher-loc><publisher-name>The Apex Press</publisher-name><source>Nurtured knowledge: learning to do participatory action research</source></citation></ref><ref id="B13"><label>13</label><citation citation-type="book"><source>Cancer mortality maps and graphs Web site [Internet]</source><publisher-loc>Bethesda (MD)</publisher-loc><publisher-name>National Cancer Institute</publisher-name><access-date>cited 12 Nov 2006</access-date><comment>Available from:<ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="http://cancer.gov/atlasplus">http://cancer.gov/atlasplus</ext-link></comment></citation></ref><ref id="B14"><label>14</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Hiatt</surname><given-names>RA</given-names></name><name><surname>Klabunde</surname><given-names>C</given-names></name><name><surname>Breen</surname><given-names>N</given-names></name><name><surname>Swan</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Ballard-Barbash</surname><given-names>R</given-names></name></person-group><year>2002</year><volume>94</volume><fpage>1837</fpage><lpage>46</lpage><article-title>Cancer screening practices from the National Health Interview Surveys: past, present, and future</article-title><source>J Natl Cancer Inst</source><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12488477</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B15"><label>15</label><citation citation-type="book"><source>The Eagle Books: stories about growing strong and preventing diabetes [Internet]</source><publisher-loc>Atlanta (GA)</publisher-loc><publisher-name>National Center for Chronic Disease Prevention and Health Promotion, Centers for Disease Control and Prevention</publisher-name><access-date>cited 2007 Jan 19</access-date><comment>Available from:<ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="http://www.cdc.gov/diabetes/pubs/eagle.htm">http://www.cdc.gov/diabetes/pubs/eagle.htm</ext-link></comment></citation></ref><ref id="B16"><label>16</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Rockhill</surname><given-names>B</given-names></name><name><surname>Kawachi</surname><given-names>I</given-names></name><name><surname>Colditz</surname><given-names>GA</given-names></name></person-group><volume>22</volume><issue>1</issue><year>2000</year><fpage>176</fpage><lpage>180</lpage><article-title>Individual risk prediction and population-wide disease prevention</article-title><source>Epidemiol Rev</source><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10939025</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B17"><label>17</label><citation citation-type="book"><source>CDC cancer prevention and control mission statement [Internet]</source><publisher-loc>Atlanta (GA)</publisher-loc><publisher-name>Centers for Disease Control and Prevention, National Center for Chronic Disease Prevention and Health Promotion, Division of Cancer Prevention and Control</publisher-name><access-date>cited 2006 Nov 15</access-date><comment>Available from:<ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="http://www.cdc.gov/cancer/dcpc/about/mission.htm">http://www.cdc.gov/cancer/dcpc/about/mission.htm</ext-link></comment></citation></ref><ref id="B18"><label>18</label><citation citation-type="book"><source>WISEWOMAN &#x02014; Well-integrated Screening and Evaluation for Women Across the Nation [Internet]</source><publisher-loc>Atlanta (GA)</publisher-loc><publisher-name>Centers for Disease Control and Prevention, National Center for Chronic Disease Prevention and Health Promotion, Division for Heart Disease and Stroke Prevention</publisher-name><access-date>cited 2006 Nov 15</access-date><comment>Available from:<ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="http://www.cdc.gov/wisewoman">http://www.cdc.gov/wisewoman</ext-link></comment></citation></ref><ref id="B19"><label>19</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Steckler</surname><given-names>AB</given-names></name><name><surname>Dawson</surname><given-names>L</given-names></name><name><surname>Israel</surname><given-names>BA</given-names></name><name><surname>Eng</surname><given-names>E</given-names></name></person-group><year>1993</year><issue>Suppl 1</issue><fpage>S3</fpage><lpage>S20</lpage><article-title>Community health development: an overview of the works of Guy W. Steuart</article-title><source>Health Educ Q</source><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8354649</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B20"><label>20</label><citation citation-type="confproc"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Burns</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Meadows</surname><given-names>D</given-names></name><name><surname>Dupuis</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name></person-group><source>Leading the way: eight American Indian communities adapting environments to promote health and prevent diabetes. Division of Diabetes Translation Conference</source><conf-loc>April 30&#x02013;May 3, 2007</conf-loc><comment>Available from:<ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="http://www.cdc.gov/diabetes">http://www.cdc.gov/diabetes</ext-link></comment></citation></ref><ref id="B21"><label>21</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Wallack</surname><given-names>L</given-names></name><name><surname>Lawrence</surname><given-names>R</given-names></name></person-group><volume>95</volume><issue>4</issue><year>2005</year><fpage>567</fpage><lpage>570</lpage><article-title>Talking about public health: developing America's "second language"</article-title><source>Am J Public Health</source><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15798110</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B22"><label>22</label><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Bird</surname><given-names>ME</given-names></name></person-group><month>2</month><year>2001</year><publisher-loc>Washington, DC</publisher-loc><publisher-name>American Public Health Association</publisher-name><source>Modern problems solved through ancient solutions. The nation's health</source></citation></ref></ref-list><fn-group><fn><p>The opinions expressed by authors contributing to this journal do not necessarily reflect the opinions of the U.S. Department of Health and Human Services, the Public Health Service, Centers for Disease Control and Prevention, or the authors' affiliated institutions. Use of trade names is for identification only and does not imply endorsement by any of the groups named above.</p></fn><fn><p>					<italic>Suggested citation for this article:</italic> Wilson KM, Satterfield DW. Where are we to be in these times? The place of chronic disease prevention in community health promotion. Prev Chronic Dis [serial online] 2007 Jul [date cited]. Available from <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="http://www.cdc.gov/pcd/issues/2007/jul/07_0014.htm">http://www.cdc.gov/pcd/issues/2007/jul/07_0014.htm</ext-link>				</p></fn></fn-group></back></article>